in Figure 2, RAG-Anything and MM-GraphRAG exhibit comparable performance on shorter documents. However, RAG-Anything’s advantages become increasingly pronounced as document length grows. On DocBench, the performance gap expands dramatically to over 13 points for documents exceeding 100 pages (68.2% vs.\nImage Path: /Users/drw/PycharmProjects/rag-anything-reproduction/parsed/rag-anything-handson-v1/mineru/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025_4ae5f35e/RAG-Anything_arXiv_2025/auto/images/f31dea98118298da9874431172c90b6519da2a5a5cb74847779484f96a73aa97.jpg\nCaptions: None\nFootnotes: None\n\nVisual Analysis: The image is a line graph titled 'DocBench Accuracy' that compares the performance of two models, 'RAGAnything' and 'MMGraphRAG', across different document lengths. The graph's x-axis is labeled 'Page Range' and is divided into five categories: '1-10', '11-50', '51-100', '101-200', and '200+'. The y-axis is labeled 'Accuracy (%)' and ranges from 0 to 100 in increments of 20. A legend in the top right corner identifies the two lines: a blue line with circular markers represents 'RAGAnything', and an orange line with circular markers represents 'MMGraphRAG'. The graph shows that for shorter documents (1-10 pages), MMGraphRAG has a slightly higher accuracy (around 65%) compared to RAGAnything (around 62%). For the 11-50 page range, both models perform similarly at approximately 62%. For the 51-100 page range, RAGAnything's accuracy remains stable at around 62%, while MMGraphRAG's accuracy drops to about 60%. A significant divergence occurs for longer documents. In the 101-200 page range, RAGAnything's accuracy increases to approximately 68%, while MMGraphRAG's accuracy drops further to around 55%. For documents over 200 pages, RAGAnything maintains its high accuracy at roughly 69%, whereas MMGraphRAG's accuracy remains low at about 55%. This graph visually supports the text's claim that RAG-Anything demonstrates superior performance on long-context documents, with the performance g